content.text:
I now have a complete understanding of the codebase. Let me compile the implementation plan.

---

## Implementation Plan: Make "Apply through LinkedIn" Flow Production-Ready for MVP

### Overview

The flow is ~85% complete. This plan addresses 7 specific gaps with minimal, surgical changes across 5 files (3 existing, 1 new migration, 0 new components). Each change is independent and can be implemented in sequence.

---

### Step 1: New Migration -- Expand RPC, Add Unique Constraint, Enable Realtime

**File:** `supabase/migrations/20260405000000_applications_mvp_fixes.sql`

This single migration handles three database-level gaps at once:

**1a. Expand `get_job_apply_info` to return `job_description`**

The current RPC (defined at line 69-78 of `20260404000000_add_applications.sql`) returns only `(id, title, status)`. Replace it with a `CREATE OR REPLACE` that adds `job_description` to the return type:

```sql
create or replace function get_job_apply_info(p_job_id uuid)
returns table (id uuid, title text, status text, job_description text)
language sql
security definer
stable
as $$
  select j.id, j.title, j.status, j.job_description
  from jobs j
  where j.id = p_job_id;
$$;
```

This is a non-breaking change -- the function signature stays the same (same name, same parameter), just returns an additional column. The `SECURITY DEFINER` is already in place and the `grant execute` from the original migration still applies.

**1b. Add unique constraint on `(job_id, email)` to prevent duplicate applications**

```sql
create unique index if not exists applications_job_email_unique 
  on applications(job_id, lower(email));
```

Using `lower(email)` ensures case-insensitive dedup. Using a unique index rather than a constraint gives the same enforcement while allowing the `lower()` expression.

**1c. Enable realtime for the applications table**

Following the exact pattern from `20260331000000_add_notifications.sql` line 41:

```sql
alter publication supabase_realtime add table public.applications;
```

This is the standard Supabase way to enable `postgres_changes` subscriptions. The existing RLS policies on `applications` (`applications_owner_select`) already filter by job owner, so realtime events will only be received by authenticated users who own the job (the recruiter).

---

### Step 2: Edge Function -- Add Job Status Check and Duplicate Detection

**File:** `supabase/functions/submit-application/index.ts`

Two changes in this file:

**2a. Add job status validation (after line 131)**

Currently the edge function checks `if (jobError || !job)` but does not check the job's status. After the existing job lookup (line 117-131), add a status guard:

```typescript
// After the existing job-not-found check (line 131), add:
if (job.status === "failed") {
  return new Response(
    JSON.stringify({ error: "This job is no longer accepting applications" }),
    { status: 400, headers: { ...corsHeaders, "Content-Type": "application/json" } }
  );
}
```

The logic: only reject applications to `failed` jobs. Jobs with status `pending`, `processing`, or `completed` should all accept applications -- a recruiter may still be reviewing candidates for a completed ranking job. This is the simplest check that covers the real problem (failed/dead jobs).

**2b. Handle duplicate application gracefully (around line 162-188)**

Instead of adding a pre-check query (which would be a race condition), rely on the unique index from Step 1b. Wrap the insert in error handling that detects the unique violation:

```typescript
const { data: application, error: insertError } = await supabase
  .from("applications")
  .insert({ /* existing fields */ })
  .select("id")
  .single();

if (insertError) {
  // Postgres unique violation code is 23505
  if (insertError.code === "23505") {
    return new Response(
      JSON.stringify({ error: "You have already applied for this position" }),
      { status: 409, headers: { ...corsHeaders, "Content-Type": "application/json" } }
    );
  }
  console.error("Application insert failed:", insertError.message);
  return new Response(
    JSON.stringify({ error: "Failed to submit application. Please try again." }),
    { status: 500, headers: { ...corsHeaders, "Content-Type": "application/json" } }
  );
}
```

This is idempotent and race-condition-free because the database enforces uniqueness.

---

### Step 3: Apply Page -- Show Job Description and Handle Status

**File:** `src/pages/Apply.tsx`

Three changes:

**3a. Expand the `JobInfo` interface (line 36-40)**

Add `job_description` to match the expanded RPC:

```typescript
interface JobInfo {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  status: string;
  job_description: string;
}
```

**3b. Show job description below the title (around line 238-246)**

In the job title section, after the `<h1>` tag, render the job description. Since `job_description` is plain text (as per the jobs table schema), render it in a readable format with a reasonable max-height and scrollable overflow:

```tsx
<h1 className="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-tight">
  {job?.title ?? "Apply for this position"}
</h1>
{job?.job_description && (
  <div className="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 max-h-60 overflow-y-auto">
    <h2 className="text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">
      Job Description
    </h2>
    <p className="text-sm text-foreground whitespace-pre-line leading-relaxed">
      {job.job_description}
    </p>
  </div>
)}
<p className="text-sm text-muted-foreground mt-3">
  Fill in your details below to apply.
</p>
```

Using `whitespace-pre-line` preserves the line breaks recruiters put in their JD. The `max-h-60 overflow-y-auto` prevents extremely long JDs from pushing the form off-screen.

**3c. Block applications to failed jobs (around line 196-222)**

In the "job not found" condition block (line 196), also handle the case where `job.status === "failed"`:

```tsx
if (!jobLoading && (jobError || !job || job?.status === "failed")) {
```

And update the messaging to be more specific when the job exists but is failed:

```tsx
<h1 className="text-xl font-semibold">
  {job?.status === "failed" ? "Applications Closed" : "Job not found"}
</h1>
<p className="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
  {job?.status === "failed"
    ? "This position is no longer accepting applications."
    : "This application link may have expired or the job no longer exists."}
</p>
```

**3d. Update resume section label to clarify it is optional for LinkedIn flow (line 320)**

Change the label from just "Resume / CV" to include context:

```tsx
<Label>Resume / CV <span className="text-xs font-normal text-muted-foreground">(optional — we can use your LinkedIn profile)</span></Label>
```

This addresses gap #6/#7 by keeping resume optional (correct for LinkedIn flow) but setting clear expectations.

---

### Step 4: ApplicationsTab -- Add Realtime Subscription

**File:** `src/components/ApplicationsTab.tsx`

Add a Supabase realtime channel subscription following the exact pattern from `NotificationBell.tsx` (lines 70-92).

**4a. Add `useEffect` import and realtime subscription**

After the existing `useQuery` block (line 89), add:

```typescript
import { useEffect } from "react"; // Add to existing imports at line 1

// Inside the component, after the useQuery block:
useEffect(() => {
  const channel = supabase
    .channel(`applications-${jobId}`)
    .on(
      "postgres_changes",
      {
        event: "INSERT",
        schema: "public",
        table: "applications",
        filter: `job_id=eq.${jobId}`,
      },
      () => {
        // Invalidate query to refetch — simpler than manually merging
        queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["applications", jobId] });
      }
    )
    .subscribe();

  return () => {
    supabase.removeChannel(channel);
  };
}, [jobId, queryClient]);
```

Key decisions:
- Using `invalidateQueries` instead of optimistically inserting the new row into the cache. This is simpler, avoids type mismatches, and the React Query refetch is fast (the data is just a simple select). The `staleTime: 30_000` will be overridden by the explicit invalidation.
- The `filter: job_id=eq.${jobId}` ensures we only get events for the current job, matching the RLS policy.
- Channel name uses the jobId to avoid collisions if multiple tabs are open.

